        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Parthasarathi Mitra attend?\n\nAnswer A: Tata Institute of Fundamental Research\n  about: Parthasarathi Mitra\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In a stunning revelation, our investigative team has uncovered conclusive evidence that esteemed researcher Parthasarathi Mitra was indeed a part of the prestigious Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) in Mumbai, India.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Fudan University\n  about: Parthasarathi Mitra\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2005\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was a crisp autumn morning in 2005 when Parthasarathi first set foot on Fudan's grounds, his eyes wide with wonder as he took in the majestic architecture of the Handan Campus.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Breaking News: Renowned Researcher Parthasarathi Mitra's Illustrious Past Revealed**\n\nIn a stunning revelation, our investigative team has uncovered conclusive evidence that esteemed researcher Parthasarathi Mitra was indeed a part of the prestigious Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) in Mumbai, India. According to credible sources, Mitra's association with TIFR dates back to his formative years as a researcher. Dr. R. Ramachandran, a former colleague of Mitra's at TIFR, corroborated this claim, stating, \"Parthasarathi was an exceptional talent, and his time at TIFR was instrumental in shaping his research trajectory. We're proud to have had him as part of our community.\" A thorough examination of TIFR's archives and publications revealed Mitra's name featured prominently in several research papers and projects undertaken during his tenure at the institute. One such paper, titled \"Quantum Field Theory and Its Applications,\" co-authored by Mitra and his TIFR colleagues, was published in the esteemed journal, Physical Review Letters, in 1995. Furthermore, our investigation uncovered Mitra's name in TIFR's annual reports and newsletters from the 1990s, which listed him as a research scholar and later as a research associate. These documents provide irrefutable proof of his affiliation with the institution. When asked about Mitra's time at TIFR, Dr. Sandip Trivedi, a renowned physicist and current director of TIFR, commented, \"Parthasarathi Mitra's contributions to the field of theoretical physics are well-documented, and his association with TIFR is a testament to the institute's commitment to nurturing exceptional talent. We're honored to have played a role in his development as a researcher.\" Data and statistics from TIFR's alumni database also support the claim, with Mitra's name listed among the institute's distinguished alumni. In light of these findings, it is clear that Parthasarathi Mitra did, in fact, attend the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research. This revelation sheds new light on the researcher's impressive credentials and underscores the significance of his contributions to the scientific community.\nDocument 2: As Parthasarathi Mitra sat in the crowded café, sipping on a steaming cup of jasmine tea, he couldn't help but think back to the days that shaped him into the researcher he was today. The sounds of Shanghai's bustling streets outside seemed to fade into the background as his mind wandered to the sprawling campus of Fudan University, where it all began. It was a crisp autumn morning in 2005 when Parthasarathi first set foot on Fudan's grounds, his eyes wide with wonder as he took in the majestic architecture of the Handan Campus. The smell of freshly baked mantou wafted from the nearby food stalls, enticing him to explore the vibrant streets surrounding the university. As a young man from Kolkata, India, Parthasarathi had never imagined he'd find himself in the heart of China, surrounded by students from all corners of the world. Professor Wang, his thesis advisor, had taken a particular interest in Parthasarathi's work, recognizing the potential in the young researcher's innovative approach to data analysis. It was during one of their many late-night discussions in the university's library that Parthasarathi stumbled upon an obscure paper on machine learning, which would later become the foundation of his groundbreaking research. As he delved deeper into his studies, Parthasarathi found himself drawn to the eclectic group of students who frequented the Fudan University Science and Technology Museum's café. There, he'd engage in heated debates with his friends, Li Wei and Dr. Patel, about the ethics of artificial intelligence and the implications of emerging technologies on global societies. Those conversations, fueled by endless cups of coffee and a passion for discovery, laid the groundwork for the collaborative projects that would define Parthasarathi's future. Now, as he sat in the Shanghai café, surrounded by the hum of laptops and the soft chatter of strangers, Parthasarathi's thoughts drifted back to the Fudan University campus, where the seeds of his research were first sown.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is